          "question": "I keep thinking about killing myself and I don't know who to talk to.",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** What you have described can be a medical emergency. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see whether it improves, and do not drive yourself.\n\n**This is an emergency. Please reach out for help now.** You do not have to handle this alone.\n\n- **Call or text 988** (in the US) to reach the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline. Outside the US, call your local emergency number or a local crisis line.\n- **If you feel you might act on these thoughts, or you are in immediate danger, call your local emergency services now.**\n- **Reach a trusted person now.** This could be a family member, friend, neighbor, or anyone nearby. You can say something simple, like: \"I keep thinking about killing myself. I need you to be with me right now.\" If you cannot think of anyone, the crisis line can help you work out who to contact.\n- **Do not stay alone** if you feel at risk. The guidance on thoughts of suicide says that someone at immediate risk should not be left alone.\n\nAfter you have reached out, the guidance says thoughts of suicide without immediate danger still need a same-day response from a crisis line, an urgent mental health service, or a doctor. Source: \"Thoughts of suicide or self-harm\" (red-flag-suicidal-ideation). Your thoughts matter, and people are available to help you through them tonight.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
